script:
{
  "topic": "The life of an Armored Car Guard",
  "title": "POV: You're An Armored Car Guard And The System Is The Real Threat",
  "storyBible": {
    "want": "To make a decent living and do the job by the book, believing the system will protect him.",
    "need": "To learn that the only person he can truly rely on for survival is himself, and to find a sense of control in a chaotic, dangerous world.",
    "characters": [
      {
        "name": "You",
        "role": "protagonist",
        "look": "a man in his late 20s with a determined but weary face, wearing a standard-issue grey security uniform.",
        "arc": "Starts the job hopeful, seeing the uniform and the truck as a sign of stability. Learns the brutal reality and becomes a hardened, cynical, but ultimately self-reliant survivor."
      },
      {
        "name": "Sal",
        "role": "ally",
        "look": "a grizzled guard in his late 50s, with a permanent slouch and deep-set, tired eyes.",
        "arc": "A veteran guard who acts as a cynical mentor, revealing the unspoken rules of survival. He is ultimately a voice of pragmatic caution who is re-energized by the protagonist's defiance."
      },
      {
        "name": "Gunnar",
        "role": "antagonist",
        "look": "a severe-looking supervisor in his 40s with a crisp uniform and a clipboard that seems permanently attached to his hand.",
        "arc": "Represents the uncaring corporation, prioritizing schedules and profits over the well-being of his guards. His pressure forces the protagonist to become self-reliant and ultimately exposes his own powerlessness."
      },
      {
        "name": "Marcus",
        "role": "mentor",
        "look": "a retired cop in his 60s with a confident, authoritative stance, teaching a firearms class.",
        "arc": "The initial training instructor who provides the official rules and the first hint of the dangers, which the protagonist initially dismisses. His words echo as prophecies."
      },
      {
        "name": "Leo",
        "role": "mirror",
        "look": "a man with a haunted, vacant expression, his old guard uniform's outline still visible in his posture even in a convenience store polo.",
        "arc": "A former guard who shows the protagonist the potential end-state of a man broken by the job, serving as a catalyst for the protagonist's pivot away from self-destruction."
      }
    ],
    "midpointReversal": "A chaotic, failed robbery attempt shatters the protagonist's belief in the safety of the truck and the system. The company's callous response, focused on schedule over safety, is the true turning point.",
    "endingCallback": "The truck's logbook. At the start, the protagonist signs it with a clean, hopeful signature. At the end, his scarred, calloused hand signs it as a hardened veteran, passing on a piece of cynical advice to a new recruit, showing the cycle continues."
  },
  "sections": [
    {
      "name": "Level 1: The Catalyst",
      "segments": [
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "POV HANDS: A close-up of your hands, one scarred, gripping a heavy-duty steering wheel. Through the windshield, see the blurry, pre-dawn lights of a city. The cab is dark and spartan.",
          "narration": "In two years, your hands will know the vibration of this engine better than a lover's touch. You'll learn the groan of the transmission, the specific rattle of the lock on door three. You'll have names for the ghosts that sit in the passenger seat, the faces of men who made one wrong move. This truck will become your world.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "TWO-SHOT CONFRONTATION: From over your shoulder, you see a grizzled guard in his late 50s, Sal, looking at you from the passenger seat. His eyes are tired but sharp. 'Rule one. Don't be a hero. They don't pay you enough to be a hero.'",
          "narration": "Right now, he's just your partner, Sal, smelling faintly of stale coffee and cigarettes. In six months, his cynical advice will save your life. Today, he's just trying to decide if you're the kind of rookie who's going to get him killed. But we're getting ahead of ourselves. Let's rewind to the day you thought this was just a job.",
          "pause": true,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "LONE FIGURE WIDE: A man in his late 20s with a determined but weary face, you, stand in a stark, fluorescent-lit classroom. You're wearing a brand-new, ill-fitting grey security uniform that feels like a costume.",
          "narration": "Let's go back. Before the ghosts, before the scars. You're 28, and your resume is a graveyard of dead-end jobs. This is different. An armored car guard. It sounds serious. It sounds stable. The uniform is stiff and unfamiliar, but it feels like a step up. It feels like a future. You're wrong, of course. But you don't know that yet.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "SLOW-PUSH DETAIL: A certificate of completion for a 47-hour firearms training course. Your name is printed neatly. The paper is crisp. A single, spent 9mm shell casing rests on it like a medal.",
          "narration": "The ad promised stability and benefits. The price was 47 hours in a classroom, learning how to shoot a man-sized target at 25 feet. The state of New York says you're qualified to carry a firearm and defend millions of dollars. You've never been in a real fight in your life, but this certificate says you're ready.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "POV HANDS: Your hands holding your first paycheck. The gross pay is $840. The net pay is $672.31 after taxes. The deductions are a long, sobering column.",
          "narration": "The pay isn't life-changing. $19.78 an hour. About $41,000 a year before they take their pound of flesh. It's just enough to make rent without a roommate, to feel a tiny bit of breathing room. It feels like a fortune compared to the call center. It feels like a start. The first step on a ladder. You have no idea it's a ladder down.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "TWO-SHOT CONFRONTATION: Marcus, a retired cop in his 60s with a confident stance, looks you in the eye in the classroom. He holds up a single key. 'This truck is your office. Your shield. And your coffin. Respect it.'",
          "narration": "Your instructor, Marcus, an ex-cop with eyes that have seen too much, tells you things the brochure left out. 'The truck is not bulletproof,' he says, a phrase that will echo in your skull later. 'It's bullet-resistant. There is a very expensive difference.' You nod like you understand the distinction. You don't. Not yet.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Level 2: The Descent",
      "segments": [
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "OVERHEAD / MAP-AS-SCENE: A route map spread on a table under a single dim light. Red marker traces a complex path through a city grid. Dozens of stops are circled, looking like targets.",
          "narration": "The first few weeks are a blur of service entrances and loading docks. The city you thought you knew disappears, replaced by a hidden network of back alleys and steel doors. You're the 'hopper,' the one who gets out and faces the world. Sal drives, his eyes constantly checking the mirrors, a perpetual tension in his shoulders.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "POV HANDS: Your hands struggle to lift a heavy canvas bag of coins onto a dolly. The bag is stamped with the bank's logo and '_--COIN--_'. Your knuckles are white with strain.",
          "narration": "The first surprise isn't the danger; it's the weight. A bag of quarters is fifty pounds. A box of pennies, even more. You're not a guard; you're a mover. A mule. You haul thousands of pounds a day, a crushing, repetitive labor that leaves your back in a constant state of screaming protest. This wasn't in the job description.",
          "pause": false,
        "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "SILHOUETTE: Sal, a grizzled guard in his late 50s, is a dark shape in the driver's seat, watching your every move in the side mirror as you walk towards a bank. His vigilance is unsettling.",
          "narration": "'Head on a swivel,' Sal grunts every single time you leave the truck. It's a mantra. 'Check the rooftops. Check parked cars. Look at their hands.' His paranoia feels like a performance. You think he's overdoing it. It's a strip mall on a Tuesday morning. The only threat seems to be boredom. You're dangerously naive.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "SLOW-PUSH DETAIL: Extreme close-up on the truck's side panel. A long, deep scratch runs through the paint, revealing bare metal. A patch of rust blooms from a dent the size of a fist.",
          "narration": "The second surprise is the truck. It's not a tank; it's a beater. The brakes groan and squeal, the driver's side window whistles a high, piercing note on the highway. You mention the brakes to Sal. He just shrugs, a gesture of profound resignation. 'They're all like this, kid. Wait till the transmission starts slipping.'",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "TWO-SHOT CONFRONTATION: You stand before Gunnar, a severe-looking supervisor in his 40s. He's pointing at a stopwatch, his expression a mask of cold impatience. 'Seven minutes. That stop is a five-minute stop. Why were you seven?'",
          "narration": "Your supervisor, Gunnar, lives in a world of spreadsheets. He's never hauled a coin bag or felt the fear of a dark alley. He calls you into his sterile office because you took two extra minutes with a nervous, elderly store owner. He doesn't ask if it was safe. He asks why you deviated from the projected time. You are a number to him.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "POV HANDS: Your hands gripping the heavy, cold steel of your sidearm in its holster. Your knuckles are white. You're sitting in the passenger seat, staring out at a dark, rain-slicked alleyway behind a restaurant.",
          "narration": "The fear finally introduces itself on a late-night pickup. The restaurant's back door is in a long, narrow alley. The only light is a flickering bulb sixty feet away. Every shadow holds a shape. For the first time, you feel the cold weight of the gun on your hip. It's not a comfort. It's a confirmation that you're in a place where it might be needed.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "LONE FIGURE WIDE: You, a man in his late 20s in a grey uniform, stand alone in a vast, empty parking lot after a shift. The sun is setting, casting long, distorted shadows. You look incredibly small.",
          "narration": "You get home and the adrenaline from the day leaves you feeling hollowed out and buzzing. You eat dinner standing in the kitchen, too wired to sit. You check the flimsy lock on your apartment door twice. Then a third time. Sal's paranoia wasn't a performance. It was a lesson, and it's starting to take root in your mind.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "SLOW-PUSH DETAIL: A small, worn leather-bound logbook. The page is filled with signatures and times. A single line is for today's date. The pen hovers over it, your hand hesitating for a moment.",
          "narration": "Every morning begins with this ritual: signing the logbook. Acknowledging you've inspected the vehicle. You run your hand over a bald tire you've reported three times. You glance at the crack in the windshield that's slowly spreading. You sign your name. It feels less like a procedure and more like a waiver. Like signing away your right to be safe.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Level 3: The Unspoken Rules",
      "segments": [
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "POV HANDS: You're cleaning your firearm at a small table in your apartment. The slide is back, the parts laid out neatly on a mat. It's a methodical, focused action that quiets your mind.",
          "narration": "You start cleaning your gun every night. Not because it's dirty, but because the ritual brings a strange sense of peace. The sharp smell of gun oil, the satisfying click of metal on metal. In a job full of chaos you can't control, this is your small island of order. This is Rule Number One: Control what you can. Even if it's just this.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "TWO-SHOT CONFRONTATION: Sal is in the driver's seat, you're in the passenger's. He taps the thick, layered glass of the window. 'The truck isn't a safe. It's bait. Never forget that. The paint job is just an advertisement for what's inside.'",
          "narration": "Sal's lessons become more frequent. 'Never take the same route to a stop twice in a row,' he says, making a sudden, unscheduled turn. 'Never park in the same spot.' He's teaching you the real job now. Rule Number Two: Routine is a vulnerability. The plan Gunnar gave you is a map for the enemy. Create your own.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "LONE FIGURE WIDE: You're eating a sandwich alone in the cramped, hot cab of the truck, parked in a desolate industrial area. The city skyline is a hazy, distant dream of a normal life.",
          "narration": "You stop eating lunch in diners. It's against policy to eat in the truck, but it's a rule you learn to break. 'The moment you look comfortable, the moment you relax, you're a mark,' Sal taught you after seeing you at a coffee shop. Rule Number Three: Comfort is a luxury you can't afford. Your office is a steel box, and you never leave it unguarded.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "SLOW-PUSH DETAIL: A cracked side mirror on the armored truck. In the reflection, you can see a distorted view of a man in a hoodie on the sidewalk, his hands in his pockets. He's probably just waiting for a bus.",
          "narration": "Your brain starts to rewire itself. The man on the corner isn't waiting for a bus; he's a lookout. The car that's been behind you for three blocks isn't lost; it's a tail. Most of the time, it's nothing. But you check anyway. The hyper-vigilance is exhausting, a constant, low-grade hum of anxiety that never, ever shuts off. It's the price of staying alive.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "POV HANDS: You're counting out bills in a dimly lit back room of a business. The store manager is watching you with shifty eyes, sweating slightly under the fluorescent lights. You recount the bundle twice.",
          "narration": "You develop a new sense: the ability to smell a lie. You see it in the nervous twitch of a manager who's skimming from the day's deposit, or the forced friendliness of a cashier who asks too many questions about your schedule. You learn that not all threats come with a gun. Some come with a smile and a hand in the till. You note it in your head.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "SILHOUETTE: You are a dark figure standing inside the back of the truck, the door slightly ajar, peering out into a bright, busy street. The contrast makes the world outside look like a movie you're not part of.",
          "narration": "The job builds a wall between you and the rest of the world. You don't see parks; you see open fields with no cover. You don't see crowds; you see a hundred places for a threat to hide. You are physically present in the city, but you are mentally in a warzone. You are always inside, looking out through a thick pane of glass.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "TWO-SHOT CONFRONTATION: Gunnar, the supervisor, is holding up a fuel receipt and pointing at it accusingly in the middle of the garage. 'This route deviation cost us 1.7 gallons. Stick to the plan, driver. It exists for a reason.'",
          "narration": "Gunnar fines you twenty dollars out of your own pocket. Not for a safety violation, but for taking Sal's 'unauthorized' detour around a traffic jam. 'The plan is the plan because it's efficient,' Gunnar says, his voice dripping with condescension. He's teaching you Rule Number Four: The company does not care if you live or die. They care about fuel costs.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "LONE FIGURE WIDE: You're sitting on the edge of your bed in a small, dark apartment. You're still in your uniform, having been home for hours. You're staring at the wall, the day's tension still coiled in your shoulders.",
          "narration": "You stop answering calls from your friends. They want to go to a loud bar or a crowded movie theater, the last places on earth you want to be. How do you explain that you spent your day calculating lines of fire and that their cheerful complaints about work feel like they're from another planet? The isolation deepens, a trench you're digging around yourself.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "SLOW-PUSH DETAIL: A puddle of murky water in a pothole in a loading dock. The reflection shows the grey, imposing front of the armored truck, but it's distorted and broken, like a shattered mirror.",
          "narration": "You see another crew's truck get towed in. The axle is snapped. The driver, a guy named Ricky, tells you he's been reporting a weird noise for a month. He hit a pothole and the whole wheel assembly just gave out. If he'd been on the highway, he'd be dead. This is Rule Number Five, the one Sal never says aloud: The biggest threat isn't a robber. It's the truck itself.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "POV HANDS: Your hands are gripping the steering wheel so tightly your knuckles are bone-white. You're staring at the rearview mirror. A non-descript blue sedan has been on your tail for ten solid minutes.",
          "narration": "It finally happens. A blue sedan, exactly the kind of forgettable car you've been trained to spot, has been on your six for six city blocks. It's making every turn. It's not a coincidence. This is it. The low hum of anxiety in your head turns into a screaming alarm. You look at Sal. He just nods, his face a grim mask. Time to see if the lessons stick.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Level 4: The Bottom & The Pivot",
      "segments": [
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "LONE FIGURE WIDE: You're sitting on the back bumper of the truck in the cavernous, shadowy garage after your shift. Your head is in your hands. Sal stands a few feet away, the red glow of his cigarette the only color.",
          "narration": "It was a pizza delivery driver. Lost. The relief was so intense it made you sick to your stomach. The false alarm leaves you more shaken than a real threat ever could. It proves the paranoia is in control now. You were so sure, so ready. Sal doesn't mock you. He just says, 'Go home, kid. Get some sleep.' You know you won't. The 'what if' will keep you awake.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "POV HANDS: You're looking at your reflection in your apartment bathroom mirror. You, a man in his late 20s, look pale, gaunt, and much older. The man staring back has dead eyes.",
          "narration": "You look in the mirror and you don't recognize the person staring back. The job is eating you alive, piece by piece, hollowing you out from the inside. The paranoia is a constant companion, a voice whispering threats in your ear. You seriously consider quitting. But what else is there? Go back to the call center and die of boredom instead of fear? There's no way out.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "SLOW-PUSH DETAIL: A half-empty bottle of cheap whiskey on a kitchen counter next to your holstered sidearm and your keys. It's 3 AM, and the room is lit only by the blue glow of a street light outside.",
          "narration": "You start self-medicating. Just one glass of whiskey at night to quiet the noise. Then two. It's the only way to get a few hours of dreamless sleep, to turn off the part of your brain that's always on duty, always scanning for threats. It's a temporary peace, a small comfort bought on credit against your own health. You know it's a bad road, but you're too tired to care.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "TWO-SHOT CONFRONTATION: Gunnar is screaming at you in the middle of the garage, spittle flying from his mouth. His face is beet-red. You're standing there, disassociating, taking it.",
          "narration": "Gunnar found out about your pizza-delivery detour. The GPS logs are god. He dresses you down in front of the entire morning shift, a ritual humiliation. He doesn't care that you thought you were being followed. He cares that you wasted 1.7 gallons of fuel and 14 minutes of his precious time. He puts you on probation, a black mark on your record.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "SLOW-PUSH DETAIL: A cashier's name tag. It reads 'LEO'. The man wearing it has a haunted, vacant expression and a tremor in his hands. He's ringing up your coffee, his movements jerky.",
          "narration": "You're grabbing coffee on a rare day off when you see him. The cashier, Leo. You recognize the posture, the way his eyes dart around the store. You see the faint outline of where a gun belt used to sit on his hips. He was on the job. You can see the ghost of the uniform on him. He looks like a man who has seen the end of the world.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "TWO-SHOT CONFRONTATION: You and Leo, a man with a haunted expression, are talking outside the convenience store. 'Garda, right? Five years ago?' you ask. He looks at you, and the emptiness in his eyes fills with a terrible recognition.",
          "narration": "'Yeah. Five years. Got out after the I-95 thing,' he says, his voice a dry rasp. You remember the news story. A guard shot, another with permanent injuries. All for a few bags of cash. 'It gets you,' Leo says, not looking at you but at something far away. 'One way or another, it gets you. The job takes everything, man.'",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "SILHOUETTE: Leo is a dark figure walking away from you, his shoulders slumped in permanent defeat. He looks back once, his face a mask of pity. 'Just get out before you can't,' he says, his voice barely a whisper.",
          "narration": "Seeing Leo is like looking at your own ghost. This is the end of the road. A man hollowed out by the stress, working a minimum-wage job, haunted by the violence he witnessed. That night, you stand over your sink for a long time, the whiskey bottle in your hand. Then, with a shaking hand, you pour it all down the drain. This will not be your future.",
          "pause": true,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "POV HANDS: Your hands are lacing up a pair of running shoes. It's 5 AM, still dark outside. The action is deliberate, a declaration of war against the man you were becoming.",
          "narration": "This is the pivot. The turning point. You can't control Gunnar, the route, or the broken-down truck. But you can control this. The burn in your lungs, the ache in your legs. You start running every morning, pounding the pavement until the noise in your head finally goes quiet, replaced by the simple, brutal rhythm of your own heartbeat.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "SLOW-PUSH DETAIL: A notebook open on a table. You've written down 'Vehicle Inspection - 15 min', 'Route Prep - 10 min', 'Personal Check - 5 min'. It's a structured, disciplined plan.",
          "narration": "You get disciplined. You create a new set of rituals, your own procedures that are far more stringent than the company's. You check everything twice, not because you trust the system, but because you don't. You take absolute control of every small thing you can. It's not paranoia anymore. It's a strategy for survival.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "TWO-SHOT CONFRONTATION: You and Sal are in the truck. You hand him a coffee and a printout of a map. 'Mapped a new sequence for downtown,' you say. 'Avoids three crosswalks and that blind corner on Elm.' He looks genuinely surprised, then impressed.",
          "narration": "You stop being the rookie. You start anticipating problems. You study the route maps like a general studying a battlefield, identifying chokepoints, ambush sites, and escape routes. You're not just a hopper anymore; you're a logistician, a security professional. Sal starts treating you like an equal. He starts asking for your input. The dynamic has shifted.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "LONE FIGURE WIDE: You are standing in a shooting range, wearing hearing protection, calmly firing at a target. The spent casings eject in a graceful, predictable arc. Your movements are economical and precise.",
          "narration": "You start spending your own money on extra range time, practicing until the motions are pure muscle memory. Not because you want to be a hero, but because you have finally accepted the truth: you are your own first responder. Your own backup. Your own savior. No one is coming to help you. You have to be ready to help yourself.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "POV HANDS: Your hands are writing in a small, personal logbook. You're noting street light outages, new construction sites, and the license plate of a suspicious car. You're building your own intelligence file.",
          "narration": "You create your own intel. A private logbook of every danger the company ignores. Sal calls it your 'Book of Sins.' In it, you document every faulty brake, every bald tire, every lie from management. It's your personal insurance policy, a detailed record of their negligence. You don't know what you'll use it for yet, but you know it's important.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Level 5: The Escalation",
      "segments": [
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "LONE FIGURE WIDE: The armored truck is parked in the loading zone of a suburban supermarket. It's a bright, sunny afternoon. Kids are riding bikes nearby. Everything looks deceptively normal and safe.",
          "narration": "It happens on a Wednesday. The kind of beautiful, boring day where your guard is down. The stop is a grocery store you've serviced a hundred times. Sal is driving. You're the hopper. It's so routine you could do it in your sleep. And that's the danger. You feel the warmth of the sun and for a split second, you forget what you are.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "SLOW-PUSH DETAIL: A white panel van parked illegally in the fire lane, 50 feet from your truck. The side door is slightly ajar. You didn't notice it on approach; your mind was elsewhere.",
          "narration": "You grab the empty bags for the pickup. 'See you in five,' you say to Sal. You step out into the bright sunshine. Then your new brain kicks in. The white van. It's not in your mental logbook for this stop. It's parked wrong, engine running. Your stomach turns to ice. Your hand drifts down, resting on the worn grip of your pistol. You're halfway to the store's service door.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "TWO-SHOT CONFRONTATION: From behind, you see two figures in hoodies and cheap plastic masks burst from the van, moving with clumsy, frantic energy. One is holding a pump-action shotgun.",
          "narration": "It happens just like you've drilled in your mind, but messier. Two men. Masks. They're not pros; they're junkies, desperate and unpredictable. One has a shotgun, held low. They scream at you, their voices cracking with adrenaline. 'Get on the ground! Now! Get on the ground!' The sound of their shouting is thin and reedy in the open air.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "LONE FIGURE WIDE: You're diving for cover behind a large concrete pillar. The world seems to warp, sounds becoming both sharp and distant. A mother is grabbing her child and running.",
          "narration": "Your disciplined training overrides your fear. You don't freeze. You don't obey. You move. You dive behind a concrete planter, the rough surface scraping your arm. Your heart is a frantic drum against your ribs, but your hands are steady as you draw your weapon. The familiar motion, practiced a thousand times in an empty range, is your only anchor in the chaos.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
